{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01GMVVUvPtzqZUPZML86SiQe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#include <string.h>\n2\t\n3\t#include \"../include/address.h\"\n4\t#include \"../include/params.h\"\n5\t#include \"../include/forsx1.h\"\n6\t#include \"../include/thash.h\"\n7\t#include \"../include/utils.h\"\n8\t#include \"../include/utilsx1.h\"\n9\t\n10\t/*\n11\t * Generate the entire Merkle tree, computing the authentication path for\n12\t * leaf_idx, and the resulting root node using Merkle's TreeHash algorithm.\n13\t * Expects the layer and tree parts of the tree_addr to be set, as well as the\n14\t * tree type (i.e. SPX_ADDR_TYPE_HASHTREE or SPX_ADDR_TYPE_FORSTREE)\n15\t *\n16\t * This expects tree_addr to be initialized to the addr structures for the\n17\t * Merkle tree nodes\n18\t *\n19\t * Applies the offset idx_offset to indices before building addresses, so that\n20\t * it is possible to continue counting indices across trees.\n21\t *\n22\t * This works by using the standard Merkle tree building algorithm,\n23\t */\n24\tvoid wots_treehashx1(unsigned char *root, unsigned char *auth_path,\n25\t\t\t     const spx_ctx* ctx,\n26\t\t\t     uint32_t leaf_idx, uint32_t idx_offset,\n27\t\t\t     uint32_t tree_height,\n28\t\t\t     uint32_t tree_addr[8],\n29\t\t\t     leaf_info_x1 *info)\n30\t{\n31\t    /* This is where we keep the intermediate nodes */\n32\t    SPX_VLA(uint8_t, stack, tree_height*SPX_N);\n33\t\n34\t    uint32_t idx;\n35\t    uint32_t max_idx = (uint32_t)((1 << tree_height) - 1);\n36\t    for (idx = 0;; idx++) {\n37\t        unsigned char current[2*SPX_N];   /* Current logical node is at */\n38\t            /* index[SPX_N].  We do this to minimize the number of copies */\n39\t            /* needed during a thash */\n40\t        wots_gen_leafx1( &current[SPX_N], ctx, idx + idx_offset,\n41\t\t\t\t info );\n42\t\n43\t        /* Now combine the freshly generated right node with previously */\n44\t        /* generate