functions.** `shake128*`, `sha3_256*`, and `sha3_512*` are declared in `fips202.h` but have no C definition anywhere in `c_src` (verified by grep — nothing calls them either). Since the required API mandates them, I implemented them from the same upstream (PQClean/SUPERCOP) source this file is derived from, reusing the exact `keccak_absorb`/`keccak_squeezeblocks`/`keccak_inc_*` helpers translated from the C. They are unreachable from SPHINCS+ code, so they cannot affect byte-identity.\n- **`SPX_thash` is defined only in `thash_shake.rs`.** The task description asked for it in both `hash_shake.rs` and `thash_shake.rs`; emitting it twice is a duplicate-symbol error, so it lives in `thash_shake.rs` (outside the cfg blocks) as that spec was explicit.\n- **VLA handling in `thash`**: the C uses `SPX_VLA` for `buf`/`bitmask`. `inblocks` is bounded by `max(SPX_WOTS_LEN, SPX_FORS_TREES)` at every call site (confirmed by grepping all `thash(` calls in `app/src`), so I use fixed stack arrays of that bound and touch only the leading `inblocks * SPX_N` bytes — no heap allocation.\n- **Aliasing**: `wots.c:36`, `utils.c:83`, `wotsx1.c:67` call `thash(x, x, …)`. Both `thash` variants read all of `input` into `buf` before writing `out`, per the requirement. The `SPX_thash` C-ABI wrapper additionally copies the input into a local buffer first, so handing out `&mut out` alongside `&input` is sound rather than aliasing UB.\n- **Shift-overflow guards**: `hash_message` uses `wrapping_shr` for `(~0u64) >> (64 - SPX_TREE_BITS)` and `(~0u32) >> (32 - SPX_LEAF_BITS)`. Rust compiles both arms of `if SPX_D == 1`, so a plain `>>` would be a compile-time overflow error when `SPX_TREE_BITS == 0`; the dead arm's value is irrelevant and the live arm is unchanged.\n- `ROL` is translated as a true 64-bit rotate (every call site uses an amount in 1..=62, so the C `(a<<n)^(a>>(64-n))` is exactly a rotate — no UB path is reachable)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":