{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a9a93603732935d3f","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01QTTpf9ghYJ8zwdkm3QDPFT","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-Qu3Xfc/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-NMiSQO/eb87ec4e-54f9-48e5-a5c6-d85aefaf339c/tasks/a9a93603732935d3f.output","summary":"## Summary\n\nBoth bugs are fixed. Only `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation/src/backend/hash_blake.rs` needed changes — `blake256.rs` and `blake512.rs` already had everything required and were **not** modified.\n\n### Diff applied (all in `hash_blake.rs`)\n\n**BUG 1 — bit-count vs byte-count.** In both `cfg` arms of `mod blakex`, the re-export `blakeX_update as blakex_update` was replaced with the bit-count entry point:\n- `blake512_update_bits as blakex_update_bits` (SPX_N >= 24 arm)\n- `blake256_update_bits as blakex_update_bits` (else arm)\n\nand all five call sites now pass the *exact* numeric value the C passes:\n\n```rust\n// gen_message_random\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N],  SPX_N as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &optrand[..SPX_N], SPX_N as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, m,                 m.len() as u64);\n// hash_message\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &r[..SPX_N],           SPX_N as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &pk[..SPX_PK_BYTES],   SPX_PK_BYTES as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, m,                     m.len() as u64);\n```\nPlus doc comments recording that the C reference feeds byte counts to a bit-count API, so only `len/8` bytes are absorbed.\n\n**BUG 2 — out-of-bounds write.** `blakex_final(&mut s, &mut r[..SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES])` replaced with finalize-into-local + truncating copy, guarded by an 8-line comment explaining the C spill is overwritten by the FORS signature and is therefore byte-identical:\n```rust\nlet mut outbuf = [0u8; SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES];\nblakex_final(&mut s, &mut outbuf);\nlet n = core::cmp::min(r.len(), SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES);\nr[..n].copy_from_slice(&outbuf[..n]);\n```\n\n### Verifications performed\n- `blake512_update_bits(&mut BlakeState512, &[u8], u64)` **already existed** with exactly the requested signature; `blake512_update` already delegates with `len*8`. No additions needed. Its body matches `c_src/lib/blake/src/blake512.c` line-for-line (`(datalen>>3)&0x7F`, `t[0] += 1024`, 1024-bit block loop, `(left<<3)+datalen`), as does `blake256_update_bits` vs `blake256.c` (`&0x3F`, 512-bit blocks, unmasked `datalen>>3` in the tail — the C is unmasked there too).\n- **No panic risk on the bit path**: both `_bits` bodies touch at most `data[..datalen>>3]` bytes, and every slice we pass is at least `8x` longer than what is consumed, so over-long slices are harmless.\n- **mgf1 confirmed byte-based, no change needed**: `blake256_mgf1`/`blake512_mgf1` in C call `blake256(...)`/`blake512(...)`, which do the `inlen*8` conversion internally. The Rust `blakex_mgf1(&mut buf[..SPX_DGST_BYTES], &seed)` with `seed.len() == 2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES` already matches C's `(bufp, SPX_DGST_BYTES, seed, 2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES)` exactly.\n\n### Results\n\nNo panics, exit 0 in all cases:\n\n| features | digest |\n|---|---|\n| blake,robust,128f | `43CB889AC09A587680B16E6B8D229EDEF772E337FF879E88B8645B0E10AE8E49` |\n| blake,simple,128f | `525D1D8AA5BDA8E4A10458C7E62B94C4CEC469BC7A994FB06F44AB6D831569EE` |\n| blake,robust,192f | `F245648DAC6BB1AC08F628761B437702E903E2714EBA9AF87D7276EDA3ED2489` |\n| blake,simple,256f | `4E2C939FD041AC10E5401B34EA7B67A61B9AD1993A16942EEA6AB74E58488DE7` |\n\nAll **12** blake combinations (`{robust,simple} x {128,192,256}{s,f}`) compile cleanly with **0 warnings**.\n\n### Two things for the coordinator to know\n1. When I first opened `hash_blake.rs` it had the byte-count calls; on re-read moments later (after an Edit rejected with \"file has been modified since read\") BUG 1's fix was **already present**, character-for-character what I intended. So something else wrote that file concurrently. I verified the content is correct and then applied BUG 2 on top. Final state is correct and stable, but you may want to confirm no other agent is also assigned to `hash_blake.rs`.\n2. Related to that: my very first `blake,robust,128f` run printed `9B677C76...`, and every run since (7+ rebuilds, including after wiping `target/release/.fingerprint` and after feature-switching) prints `43CB889A...` deterministically with no source file newer than my edit. I could not reproduce `9B677C76`, so I attribute it to the concurrent-write window above. `src/main.rs` still uses the byte-based `blake256_update`/`blake512_update` slice wrappers (lines 47 and 75), i.e. the driver's own transcript bug is still unfixed, so none of these digests should be expected to match `reference_outputs.txt` yet (which has C values only for `blake robust 128s` = `373DBB13...` and `blake robust 192s` = `A61C8A41...`).","usage":{"total_tokens":53893,"tool_uses":25,"duration_ms":270754},"uuid":"52eaa421-1258-4e53-a36c-9a338b73f560","session_id":"eb87ec4e-54f9-48e5-a5c6-d85aefaf339c"}
