ure; `blake512_update` already delegates with `len*8`. No additions needed. Its body matches `c_src/lib/blake/src/blake512.c` line-for-line (`(datalen>>3)&0x7F`, `t[0] += 1024`, 1024-bit block loop, `(left<<3)+datalen`), as does `blake256_update_bits` vs `blake256.c` (`&0x3F`, 512-bit blocks, unmasked `datalen>>3` in the tail — the C is unmasked there too).\n- **No panic risk on the bit path**: both `_bits` bodies touch at most `data[..datalen>>3]` bytes, and every slice we pass is at least `8x` longer than what is consumed, so over-long slices are harmless.\n- **mgf1 confirmed byte-based, no change needed**: `blake256_mgf1`/`blake512_mgf1` in C call `blake256(...)`/`blake512(...)`, which do the `inlen*8` conversion internally. The Rust `blakex_mgf1(&mut buf[..SPX_DGST_BYTES], &seed)` with `seed.len() == 2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES` already matches C's `(bufp, SPX_DGST_BYTES, seed, 2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES)` exactly.\n\n### Results\n\nNo panics, exit 0 in all cases:\n\n| features | digest |\n|---|---|\n| blake,robust,128f | `43CB889AC09A587680B16E6B8D229EDEF772E337FF879E88B8645B0E10AE8E49` |\n| blake,simple,128f | `525D1D8AA5BDA8E4A10458C7E62B94C4CEC469BC7A994FB06F44AB6D831569EE` |\n| blake,robust,192f | `F245648DAC6BB1AC08F628761B437702E903E2714EBA9AF87D7276EDA3ED2489` |\n| blake,simple,256f | `4E2C939FD041AC10E5401B34EA7B67A61B9AD1993A16942EEA6AB74E58488DE7` |\n\nAll **12** blake combinations (`{robust,simple} x {128,192,256}{s,f}`) compile cleanly with **0 warnings**.\n\n### Two things for the coordinator to know\n1. When I first opened `hash_blake.rs` it had the byte-count calls; on re-read moments later (after an Edit rejected with \"file has been modified since read\") BUG 1's fix was **already present**, character-for-character what I intended. So something else wrote that file concurrently. I verified the content is correct and then applied BUG 2 on top. Final state is correct and stable, but you may want to confirm no other agent is also assigned to `hash_blake.rs`.\n2. Related to that: my very first `blake,robust,128f` run printed `9B677C76...`, and every run since (7+ rebuilds, including after wiping `target/release/.fingerprint` and after feature-switching) prints `43CB889A...` deterministically with no source file newer than my edit. I could not reproduce `9B677C76`, so I attribute it to the concurrent-write window above. `src/main.rs` still uses the byte-based `blake256_update`/`blake512_update` slice wrappers (lines 47 and 75), i.e. the driver's own transcript bug is still unfixed, so none of these digests should be expected to match `reference_outputs.txt` yet (which has C values only for `blake robust 128s` = `373DBB13...` and `blake robust 192s` = `A61C8A41...`)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9a93603732935d3f (use SendMessage with to: 'a9a93603732935d3f', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 56198\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 270755</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb87ec4e-54f9-48e5-a5c6-d85aefaf339c","uuid":"cfced069-e40b-48aa-a945-d95f79cd8ae5","timestamp":"2026-09-02T12:10:02.959Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You must fix TWO fidelity bugs in an existing Rust translation. Work ONLY in\n`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation/src/backend/hash_blake.rs`\n(and, if strictly needed, `blake256.rs` / `blake512.rs` in the same directory — but do\nNOT change the existing public signatures there, only ADD items).\nNEVER modify `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/c_src` (read-only reference),\nand do NOT touch any other Rust file (in particular NOT src/main.rs, src/sign.rs,\nsrc/lib.rs, Cargo.toml).\n\nBuild with `cargo build --release --offline --no-default-features --features \"blake,robust,128f\"`\nfrom `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation`\n(the `--offline` flag is required in this sandbox).\n\n=== BUG 1: blake*_update takes a BIT count, and hash_blake.c passes BYTE counts ===\nIn `c_src/lib/blake/src/blake256.c`:\n```c\nvoid blake256_update( blakestate256 *S, const u8 *data, u64 datalen )  /* datalen is in BITS */\nint blake256( unsigned char *out, const unsigned char *in, unsigned long long inlen )\n{ ... blake256_update( &S, in, inlen*8 ); ... }   /* <- converts bytes to bits */\n```\nBut `c_src/lib/blake/src/hash_blake.c` calls\n```c\nblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);        /* SPX_N is a BYTE count, used as a BIT count! */\nblakeX_update(&S, optrand, SPX_N);\nblakeX_update(&S, m, mlen);\nblakeX_update(&S, R, SPX_N);\nblakeX_update(&S, pk, SPX_PK_BYTES);\n```\nThis is a BUG IN THE ORIGINAL C, and we MUST reproduce it byte-for-byte, i.e. only\n`datalen >> 3` bytes of each input are actually consumed.\n\nThe current Rust `hash_blake.rs` calls `blakex_update(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N])`, whose\nslice-based wrapper does `datalen = input.len() * 8` — WRONG. Change every\n`blakex_update` call in `hash_blake.rs` (in both `gen_message_random` and\n`hash_message`) to use the explicit bit-count entry points\n`crate::backend::blake256::blake256_update_bits(state, data, bits)` /\n`crate::backend::blake512::blake512_update_bits(state, data, bits)`\npassing EXACTLY the same numeric value the C code passes (`SPX_N as u64`,\n`SPX_PK_BYTES as u64`, `m.len() as u64`).\n* First VERIFY that `blake512_update` in `blake512.rs` also interprets its length as\n  bits (check `c_src/lib/blake/src/blake512.c`: it should use `datalen >> 3`, 1024-bit\n  blocks) and that a `blake512_update_bits` exists; if only the slice form exists, ADD\n  a `pub fn blake512_update_bits(state: &mut BlakeState512, data: &[u8], datalen: u64)`\n  holding the real body and make the slice form delegate with `len*8` (mirroring how\n  blake256.rs is already structured). Do not change existing signatures.\n* Make sure the bit-count path cannot panic when `datalen/8 < data.len()` (it just\n  consumes fewer bytes) — the slices passed in are longer than what is consumed.\n* Also double-check the `blakex_mgf1` call in `hash_message`: in C it is\n  `blakeX_mgf1(bufp, SPX_DGST_BYTES, seed, 2*SPX_N + SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES)` and\n  `blake256_mgf1`/`blake512_mgf1` internally call `blake256(...)`/`blake512(...)`\n  which DO multiply by 8 — so mgf1 is byte-based and needs no change. Confirm this.\n\n=== BUG 2: gen_message_random writes past the caller's buffer ===\nC:\n```c\nvoid gen_message_random(unsigned char *R, ...) {\n  blakestateX S; blakeX_init(&S); ...; blakeX_final(&S, R);\n}\n```\n`blakeX_final` writes SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES bytes (32 for SPX_N<24, 64 otherwise),\nwhich is MORE than SPX_N. In C this overruns into the rest of the signature buffer,\nand those extra bytes are later fully overwritten by the FORS signature, so the final\noutput is unaffected.\nIn Rust, `crate::sign` passes an `r` slice of exactly SPX_N bytes and the current code\ndoes `blakex_final(&mut s, &mut r[..SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES])` which PANICS\n(\"range end index 32 out of range for slice of length 16\").\nFix by finalizing into a local `[u8; SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES]` buffer and then copying\n`min(r.len(), SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES)` bytes into `r`. Add a comment explaining that\nthe discarded bytes are overwritten by the FORS signature in the C original, so this\nis byte-identical.\n\n=== VERIFY ===\n1. `cargo build --release --offline --no-default-features --features \"blake,robust,128f\"`\n   then run `./target/release/driver` — it must print a line\n   `KAT transcript digest = <64 hex chars>` and exit 0 (no panic).\n   NOTE: the driver's own transcript hash (in src/main.rs) has the SAME bit-count bug\n   and is NOT yours to fix — the coordinator is fixing it in parallel. So the digest\n   value you see may still change later; you only need \"no panic, exit 0\".\n2. Repeat for `blake,simple,128f`, `blake,robust,192f`, `blake,simple,256f`.\n   (Avoid the `s` parameter sets — they are slow.)\n3. Confirm all 12 blake feature combinations still COMPILE.\n\nReport: the exact diff you applied (as a summary), the digests you observed, and\nconfirmation that only hash_blake.rs / blake256.rs / blake512.rs were touched.","agentId":"a9a93603732935d3f","agentType":"general-purpose","harnessNoteCount":0,"harnessTailCount":0,"harnessSectionHash":"e5efc036d5311c48","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Summary\n\nBoth bugs are fixed. Only `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation/src/backend/hash_blake.rs` needed changes — `blake256.rs` and `blake512.rs` already had everything required and were **not** modified.\n\n### Diff applied (all in `hash_blake.rs`)\n\n**BUG 1 — bit-count vs byte-count.** In both `cfg` arms of `mod blakex`, the re-export `blakeX_update as blakex_update` was replaced with the bit-count entry point:\n- `blake512_update_bits as blakex_update_bits` (SPX_N >= 24 arm)\n- `blake256_update_bits as blakex_update_bits` (else arm)\n\nand all five call sites now pass the *exact* numeric value the C passes:\n\n```rust\n// gen_message_random\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N],  SPX_N as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &optrand[..SPX_N], SPX_N as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, m,                 m.len() as u64);\n// hash_message\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &r[..SPX_N],           SPX_N as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, &pk[..SPX_PK_BYTES],   SPX_PK_BYTES as u64);\nblakex_update_bits(&mut s, m,                     m.len() as u64);\n```\nPlus doc comments recording that the C reference feeds byte counts to a bit-count API, so only `len/8` bytes are absorbed.\n\n**BUG 2 — out-of-bounds write.** `blakex_final(&mut s, &mut r[..SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES])` replaced with finalize-into-local + truncating copy, guarded by an 8-line comment explaining the C spill is overwritten by the FORS signature and is therefore byte-identical:\n```rust\nlet mut outbuf = [0u8; SPX_BLAKEX_OUTPUT_BYTES];\nblakex_final(&mut s, &mut outbuf);\nlet n = core::cmp::min(r.len(), SPX_BLAKE