{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_opo5auzjkbwpcve6z63mv3bletakzn6gu6kplfsaqo6gbqccgflq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four files are written and verified byte-identical against the C reference for all 12 blake configurations.\n\n## Files written (line counts)\n| File | Lines |\n|---|---|\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation/src/backend/blake256.rs` | 368 |\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation/src/backend/blake512.rs` | 376 |\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation/src/backend/hash_blake.rs` | 320 |\n| `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-NMiSQO/translation/src/backend/thash_blake.rs` | 167 |\n\nNo other file touched. Builds clean (zero warnings/errors from my files) for `blake,robust,128s`, `blake,simple,192s`, and the default `haraka` config.\n\n## Verification\nBuilt the C blake backend (`blake256.c`, `blake512.c`, `hash_blake.c`, `thash_blake_{robust,simple}.c`, `utils.c`, `address.c`) and a test harness covering `blake256/512` (24 input lengths incl. all padding boundaries 54/55/56, 110/111/112, 127/128/129), chunked `*_update`, `*_mgf1` (3 input sizes x 13 output lengths), `prf_addr`, `thash` (inblocks 1..8 plus `SPX_WOTS_LEN`/`SPX_FORS_TREES`), `gen_message_random` and `hash_message` (19 message lengths incl. 440/441/888/1024/3520/3521). Output compared to the Rust lib for all 12 `{128s,128f,192s,192f,256s,256f} x {robust,simple}` combinations: **all MATCH** (174 lines each). Additionally, the *C harness linked against the Rust `.so`* (exercising the `extern \"C\"` wrappers and the `blakestate256/512` struct layouts) reproduces the C output exactly.\n\n## Key findings faithfully reproduced (not fixed)\n- **`blake*_update` takes a bit count, but `hash_blake.c` passes byte counts** (`SPX_N`, `SPX_PK_BYTES`, `mlen`). Consequence: only `len/8` bytes are absorbed, and for short messages *no compression ever runs*, so `gen_message_random` returns the raw BLAKE IV (e.g. `6a09e667bb67ae85...` for 128s). I therefore expose `blake256_update_bits`/`blake512_update_bits` (used by `hash_blake`) alongside the byte-slice `blake256_update`/`blake512_update` required by the spec, and pass `SPX_N as u64` / `m.len() as u64` as *bit* counts. Getting this wrong was the only initial mismatch.\n- `prf_addr` appends `sk_seed` to `buf` but hashes only `SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES` bytes, so `sk_seed` is never covered.\n- All wrap-around preserved with `wrapping_*` (`t[0] -= 440 - buflen` underflows to a huge `u32`/`u64`; `buflen = (left<<3)+datalen` truncated to `i32`), plus blake512's missing `t[1]` carry and its redundant `& 0x7F` mask.\n- The 14/16 `ROUND` macro expansions are driven by the standard sigma table: argument *k* uses `m[sigma[k]] ^ cst[sigma[k^1]]` (constants are the pairwise swap of the message indices in every round) — verified against every `ROUND(...)` line.\n\nNote: `gen_message_random` in `hash_blake.rs` was edited on disk by someone else while I worked (it now finalizes into a local buffer and copies `min(r.len(), OUTPUT_BYTES)`). I kept it: in C the surplus `OUTPUT_BYTES - SPX_N` bytes spill past `R` into the signature buffer and are unconditionally overwritten by `fors_sign` afterwards, so it is observationally identical while avoiding an out-of-bounds panic — provided callers still pass a slice of at least `SPX_N` bytes. `SPX_thash` lives only in `thash_blake.rs` (not duplicated in `hash_blake.rs`) to avoid a duplicate symbol."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2981,"cache_read_input_tokens":154250,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2981,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01EnMv7UVEeRWAetX27huR7p","session_id":"eb87ec4e-54f9-48e5-a5c6-d85aefaf339c","uuid":"bb035498-a0f1-48e6-83e8-3bee486c09cc","timestamp":"2026-09-02T12:11:22.002Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate blake backend"}
